# Deploying with Docker

This repo ships generated Docker artifacts: `Dockerfile`, `.dockerignore`,
`docker-compose.yml`{{if .Env}}, `.env.example`{{end}}.

## VPS quickstart

On a fresh Debian or Ubuntu VPS:

1. Install Docker (engine plus the Compose plugin):

   ```sh
   curl -fsSL https://get.docker.com | sh
   ```

2. Get this repository onto the server — `git clone`, or `rsync -a` from your
   machine.
{{if .RequiredEnv}}
3. Create `.env` from the template and fill in real values:

   ```sh
   cp -n .env.example .env
   ```

   `docker-compose.yml` reads `.env` automatically for both `env_file` and
   `${VAR}` interpolation. Remove optional assignments you do not need so
   empty values do not replace the app's defaults. Keep an existing `.env`.
{{else if .Env}}
3. No environment setup is needed to use the app's defaults. With Docker
   Compose 2.24.0 or newer, the generated configuration loads `.env` only
   when it exists. To override a default, create `.env` with just the values
   you want to set. See `.env.example` for the detected variable names.
{{else}}
3. No environment variables were detected — nothing to configure. If the app
   later needs configuration, add `env_file: .env` to the app service in
   `docker-compose.yml`.
{{end}}
4. Build and start everything:

   ```sh
   docker compose up -d --build
   ```

5. Check status and logs:

   ```sh
   docker compose ps
   docker compose logs -f app
   ```

## Process notes
{{if .Web}}
The `app` service publishes port {{.Port}}. For a public deployment put a
reverse proxy in front for TLS and routing. [Caddy](https://caddyserver.com)
is the lowest-effort option — a two-line Caddyfile is enough:

```caddy
app.example.com {
	reverse_proxy localhost:{{.Port}}
}
```
{{else if .Worker}}
The `app` service is a background worker: it publishes no ports and talks to
its backing services over the compose network. `restart: unless-stopped`
keeps it running across crashes and host reboots; stop it deliberately with
`docker compose stop app`.
{{else if .Static}}
The `app` service serves prebuilt static files through nginx inside the
image. Put a CDN or a reverse proxy such as Caddy in front for caching and
TLS.
{{else}}
